### C is Exclusively Call-by-Value

In C, **all function arguments are passed by value**. This means the formal parameters receive a **copy** of the caller's actual arguments on the stack frame. Any modification to parameters inside the function has **zero effect** on the caller's original variables.

### Simulating Call-by-Reference using Pointers:
To enable a function to mutate a variable in the caller's scope, pass the **memory address** of the variable using the address-of operator `&`. Inside the function, dereference the pointer using `*`:

```c
// Classic Swap Function
void swap(int *a, int *b) {
    int temp = *a;
    *a = *b;
    *b = temp;
}

int main(void) {
    int x = 10, y = 20;
    swap(&x, &y); // Passes addresses
    // x is now 20, y is now 10!
}
```

```
Caller (main):          Callee (swap):
[ x: 10 ] (addr: 0x100) <--- [ a: 0x100 ]
[ y: 20 ] (addr: 0x104) <--- [ b: 0x104 ]
*a = 20 overwrites memory at 0x100 directly!
```

